---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import { SourceVerificationChallenge } from "../../components/SourceVerificationChallenge";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Citing Sources - AI Training Guide">
  <PageShell
    chapterId="competitive-research"
    subChapterId="citing-sources"
  >
    <div class="prose space-y-6">
      <p>
        One of the simplest things you can do to improve the quality of AI
        output is to ask it to cite its sources. Most AI tools won't include
        references unless you explicitly request them.
      </p>

      <h3>Ask for Sources Every Time</h3>

      <p>
        Adding "cite your sources" or "include source URLs" to your prompts
        is a quick and easy way to
        verify what the AI is telling you. This applies well beyond
        research. Asking about a technical approach? Request the docs it's
        referencing. Getting architecture advice? Ask where the best
        practice comes from. Writing up a competitive comparison? Ask for
        links to the pages it's pulling from.
      </p>

      <p>
        Sources turn AI output from "trust me" into "here's where I got
        this," which makes it much easier to build on with confidence.
      </p>

      <Callout variant="info" title="Add to your skills">
        <p>
          Consider adding a line like <span class="italic">"Always cite sources
          with URLs where possible"</span> to your reusable skills and
          templates. That way every prompt benefits from source tracking
          without you needing to remember to ask each time.
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <SourceVerificationChallenge client:visible />

      <h3>Verify Before You Share</h3>

      <p>
        When the AI gives you sources, treat them as leads to follow up on.
        Open the links, confirm the page exists, and check that it says what
        the AI claims. Most of the time things will check out, especially
        with web-enabled tools, but occasionally you'll find a link that's
        outdated or doesn't quite match. A quick spot-check before including
        anything in a deliverable keeps you covered.
      </p>

      <Callout variant="warning" title="Web access doesn't fix citations">
        <p>
          It's tempting to assume a tool that searches the web gets its
          sources right. A 2025 Columbia Journalism Review{" "}
          <a href="https://www.cjr.org/tow_center/we-compared-eight-ai-search-engines-theyre-all-bad-at-citing-news.php" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline">study</a>{" "}
          tested eight AI search engines and found they cited the wrong or a
          fabricated source more than 60% of the time. Even the best performer,
          Perplexity, was wrong 37% of the time, and paid tiers were often more
          confidently wrong, not less. Tools without web access will invent
          plausible URLs outright. Either way the rule is the same: click
          through and confirm before you rely on a source, whether you're using
          ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, or Perplexity.
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <h3>Beyond Research</h3>

      <p>
        Asking for citations is useful any time the AI is making factual
        claims. Writing documentation? Ask it to link to the API docs.
        Evaluating a library? Ask for the GitHub repo and changelog.
        Summarizing a competitor's positioning? Ask for the specific pages
        it's referencing. The habit of requesting sources makes all of your
        AI-assisted work easier to verify and builds a trail of references
        you can come back to later.
      </p>

     
    </div>
  </PageShell>
</MainLayout>
